<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-astronauts">
  <title>Who went up in space for how long?</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>"The sky's the limit." (Idiom)</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Human space travel has captured the world's attention since the first spaceflight by Yuri Gagarin in
      April 1961.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Questions.</em> How many men and women have gone up in space? How long were they in space?
      Which countries did they come from?
    </p>
    <p><em>Sources.</em> Tidytuesday astronauts data.</p>
    <p><em>Structure.</em> 1,277 records each of 24 variables.</p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-astro-flights">
    <title>What human spaceflights have there been?</title>

    <p>
      The Tidytuesday project includes a new dataset each week. The astronauts dataset uploaded in July 2020
      contained information on manned spaceflights up to the end of 2019. Each flight by each person is a separate
      record, making 1,277 in all. Several individuals flew more than once and there are 565 different people in
      the dataset.
    </p>
    <p>
      Human spaceflight may be defined as reaching beyond 100 km from Earth. The Challenger mission of 1986 that
      ended in disaster did not get that far but is included in the dataset; the non-fatal Soyuz mission of
      September 1983 that failed before launch is not included.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-asty">
      <image source="images/asty-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Numbers of people by year of flight.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      There are sharp drops in participant numbers after the Soyuz 1 crash in 1967, after the Challenger disaster
      in 1986, and after the Columbia disaster in 2003.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-astg">
      <image source="images/astg-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Scatterplot of age by year of flight coloured by sex, with smooths and their confidence intervals.</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Early participants were male and younger than later participants. Females were fewer and on average younger
      than males. The vertical scale extends high because of John Glenn's third spaceflight at age 77 in 1998.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-astb">
      <image source="images/astb-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Numbers of participants by decade and by sex.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Barcharts by decade and sex show the rarity of female flights in earlier decades and the high volume of
      flights in the 1990s.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-astro-mission-times">
    <title>Mission times in space</title>

    <p>
      Mission length from launch to return is a key variable. Most flights were relatively short, especially in the
      1960s. Later years contain many short flights as well as a group of long flights near six months, plus a few
      very long missions approaching or exceeding a year.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-flightt">
      <image source="images/flightt-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Scatterplot of mission time in hours by year of flight.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      A key explanatory factor is whether a mission visited a space station and, if so, which programme.
      Long flights are associated with station missions.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-flighttc">
      <image source="images/flighttc-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Mission time in hours by year of flight coloured by space station programme.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The code used to build this display also uncovered typos in station names, including misspellings of
      "Salyut". Minor typos can be irritating or misleading and are often discovered through visualization.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-astro-log-times">
    <title>Logging mission times</title>

    <p>
      Because short mission times dominate, a log transformation reveals more structure and helps with
      interpretation.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-flighttl">
      <image source="images/flighttl-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Scatterplot of log mission time by year of flight coloured by space station programme.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      On the log scale the short and long mission groups appear less variable, and the decline in shorter missions
      after 2010 becomes clearer. A few extreme low values correspond to zero or near-zero recorded mission times,
      including entries related to disasters and aborted flights, exposing inconsistencies in the data.
    </p>

    <p>
      Nationality is another major factor. Forty nationalities appear, but U.S. and U.S.S.R./Russia dominate.
      Faceting with ghosted background points helps compare groups while retaining overall context.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-flightn">
      <image source="images/flightn-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Scatterplots of log mission time by year of flight by nationality.</p></caption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-astro-checking-data">
    <title>Checking the data with graphics</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Checking whether crew members on the same mission had matching times uncovered many inconsistencies.
        Grouping by mission descriptors finds many matches but not all, because spelling, case, punctuation,
        and coding differences are present.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-difs">
        <image source="images/difs-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Boxplot of biggest differences in times for (possibly) the same missions.</p></caption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The largest discrepancies include implausibly different times for astronauts who should be on the same
        mission. The dataset illustrates why data checking is essential and why plots are effective for finding
        anomalies.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ideally, all typos and errors should be corrected. Even the first row raises concerns about internal
        consistency for Yuri Gagarin's mission coding.
      </p>
      <p>
        Exact mission dates would add precision beyond mission year and are available from external sources.
        Despite individual errors, the dataset still supports informative analysis.
      </p>

      <p>
        <em>Answers.</em> Many more men than women have gone up in space and men have been older on average.
        Early flights were short. Since space station programmes began, much longer stays became common.
        Most participants were Russians or Americans, though this is changing.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Further questions.</em> How many flights has each person made? Are there differences between
        U.S.S.R./Russian and American participants?
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-astro-takeaways">
      <title>Graphical takeaways</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Graphics uncover typos, errors, and deeper data-quality issues.</p></li>
        <li><p>Scatterplots are excellent but should be complemented when duplicate values overlap.</p></li>
        <li><p>Keep colours consistent for the same categories and vary palettes for different groupings.</p></li>
        <li><p>Log transformations help with skewed distributions when axis labels remain interpretable.</p></li>
        <li><p>Faceting disentangles overlapping groups in scatterplots.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
